      "side": 1,
      "message": "I find this solution a lot more complex than is necessary. I especially dislike the necessity for callers to know whether to clear the satisfier, as this means relying on lingering members on objects that are on their way to garbage collection, which I find extremely dangerous.\n\nCounter-proposal ❶ : how about calling incrementCountOrThrow in handleRegisterNetworkRequests immediately after put() instead of in the constructors ? I don\u0027t think any other change would be necessary. The default request would not be counted, which is almost better (or we could count it, it wouldn\u0027t matter). If I\u0027m not mistaken this would be a +1-3 lines change, instead of this +130-25 change.\n\nCounter-proposal ❷ : exempt the system server from the 100 callbacks limit. We\u0027ve been thinking about this for a while anyway. The drawback is that we won\u0027t realize if the system server leaks requests.\n\nI would favor ❶. It sounds simpler and in fact more correct than increasing the count in the constructor for NetworkRequestInfo and doesn\u0027t have the drawback of ❷.",
      "revId": "4d381afe0b6a28dcae826ac3a13a408815d679a5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7db31cb6_b3add8e0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1470463
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-29T22:50:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "❶ unfortunately won\u0027t work. \n\nI did consider this initially (it\u0027s a very good idea) however it doesn\u0027t work as handleRegisterNetworkRequests happens on the handler thread whereas current behavior throws this error synchronously.\n\nBased off of your input, I did give it a shot to see if I could call the increment method in each spot nris are created synchronously:\n    mNetworkRequestCounter.incrementCountOrThrow(mUid);\n\nFor some reason, I wasn\u0027t able to get the testNetworkCallbackMaximum() to pass when doing so and even if I was able to get that to work, as I started updating the code I realized we\u0027d have the same problem of callers of the NRI constructor to know that in each place it is created currently (and in the future), they would need to call incrementCountOrThrow. \n\nEach place an NRI is created turns out to be a relatively sizable amount vs the single spot where we currently have to worry about clearing the satisfier with the current patch therefore when weighing the two approaches, the current patch seemed to better mitigate the tradeoffs.\n\nLet me know your thoughts and we can discuss more tonight as well. Thanks!",
      "parentUuid": "9e97125f_26ba2991",
